# All text files: normalize to LF in the index.
# Declared explicitly so git never has to guess. The only reason a file is
# *not* normalized is that it is intentionally binary (assets, snapshots).
#
# Pair this with `core.eolInput=false` so the working tree is LF too (see
# `make env`). Without it, git re-emits CRLF on every checkout under
# `core.autocrlf=true`, and a pull silently rewrites every file that was not
# touched — a phantom diff that costs an AI agent a full normalisation pass.
* text=auto

# Binary: git's auto-detection gets this right already, but spell it out so a
# future `text` rule cannot ever reach into an asset.
*.png binary

# Explicit text: keep normalized even though the extension is uncommon.
# Without this git would auto-detect them as text anyway, so the real payoff
# is that a stray NUL byte in one of these files cannot flip it to binary.
*.ipynb text
*.geojson text
*.md text
*.rst text
*.yaml text
*.toml text
*.gitattributes text
*.gitignore text

# Build outputs are not checked in today, but if they are ever added they must
# be pinned as binary: they are hashed (source maps, bundles) and line-ending
# churn there is pure diff noise.
foliplus/dist/** binary
